---
/* Post bodies render through the SAME block components as pages — never Astro's own
 * markdown pipeline. Astro's markdown image handling resolves a relative path from the
 * entry file's own directory (`content/posts/<locale>/`), which is the wrong root: media
 * references are rooted at `content/`, always. So this component never calls into Astro's
 * markdown rendering at all — it parses the raw body itself with the same remark stack the
 * contract's own `InlineText` check is built on (`../contract/defs.ts`), maps the result to
 * the contract's own Block shapes, and hands the array to BlockList — one renderer, not two,
 * so the same markup contract that governs page blocks holds for prose too, with no
 * separate fixtures needed.
 *
 * Only the eight constructs our markdown subset actually produces have a mapping here:
 * heading, paragraph (or image, when a paragraph is exactly one bare image, e.g.
 * `![alt](media/name.jpg)`), list (three explicit levels, matching blocks.ts's own
 * List/ListL2/ListL3 — the cap is BY CONSTRUCTION, since a mapped fourth level has no `list`
 * field to occupy, mirroring how blocks.ts itself enforces the same cap), blockquote ->
 * quote, a fenced code block, a table, and a thematic break -> separator. The three custom
 * jamground/* types have no markdown construct and so never appear here. Content-level
 * validation — raw HTML, h1/h5–h6, fence info strings, an empty prose alt — is not this
 * renderer's job: a body reaching this component is assumed already validated, and an
 * unrecognised construct throws rather than silently dropping content.
 *
 * The canonical markdown settings below match the ones defs.ts uses for InlineText
 * (`bullet`, `emphasis`, `strong`, `fences`, `listItemIndent`, `rule`, `ruleSpaces` govern
 * InlineText scalars as well as markdown bodies), plus `resourceLink: true`, which
 * `defs.ts`'s own InlineText stringifier sets for the same reason: without it, a link whose
 * label equals its href round-trips through the autolink shorthand, which InlineText's
 * canonical-form check rejects by name. `defs.ts` builds the identical pipeline for that
 * check; it exports no stringify function of its own to reuse here (its concern is the
 * check, not a render-time serialiser), so the same settings are applied again rather than
 * imported. */
import { mdastToBlocks } from '../lib/mdast-to-blocks.ts';
import { resolveBlockMedia } from '../lib/media.ts';
import BlockList from './BlockList.astro';

type Props = { body: string };
const { body } = Astro.props as Props;

/* MEDIA IS RESOLVED HERE, and link resolution deliberately is not — the asymmetry is the point.
 * A route resolves links before it hands blocks down, and this component is not on that path;
 * the reason it does not need to be is that mdastToBlocks maps eight markdown constructs and
 * none of them is a hero or a cta, so the prose path can provably never carry a link.
 *
 * It CAN carry an image. `![alt](media/name.jpg)` is one of those eight, so a post body is a
 * second place a MediaRef enters the renderer, and it enters it here rather than in a route.
 * Resolving it anywhere else would mean either passing a resolver down or letting Image.astro
 * read the filesystem — and the whole reason this component exists is that it refuses to let a
 * media path be resolved against the wrong root. */
const blocks = resolveBlockMedia(mdastToBlocks(body));
---
<BlockList blocks={blocks} />
